 IMHO I think it's a bad idea if you leave it unlimited.

 Think, for example: you made an Amulet of Yendor + Amulet of Lifesaving fusion.

 In the way up the dungeon, you die, but the lifesaving amulet of livesaving 
saves you.

 Sorry, you lost your amulet of Yendor. And if you can't wish for it, game over 
(IMHO) :-P.

 So, there's some ideas. I think you'll find them a bit annoying, but if a game
is too easy, where's the challenge?

- In a fusion there's a strong and a weak element. The strong element gives to
  the final object the 66% of it. The weak only 50%. A element is weak or strong
  because of one of their common property (see the example). If there are no
  common property, no fusion is possible. Better, you can have a fusion only
  between objects of the same tipe.

  Ex.: a cursed surely-poison food (250 nutrition points) (a) +
       a blessed non-poison food (200 nutrition points) (b).

  Basing on NP, (a) is the strong element and (b) the weak. So you'll have:

       a uncursed 66%-poison food (265 nutrition points).

  265 = (250 * 0.66) + (200 * 0.5)

- You can't have a fusion with a game-crytical object (i.e. amulet of Yendor)

- The fusion has a probability of success depending on how the starting object
  as similar. Two foods are very likely to mix. A food and a ring not.

- You can have a fusion of max 3 elements.

- And so on.

